{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794373191072,"sessionID":"ses_e370b5ae4ffe9rbeud9vtYO6Dm","part":{"id":"prt_1c8fa0588001xsmE2KPeZlBGXN","messageID":"msg_1c8f9d573001ffMPpxzTUYPUsc","sessionID":"ses_e370b5ae4ffe9rbeud9vtYO6Dm","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794373191072,"sessionID":"ses_e370b5ae4ffe9rbeud9vtYO6Dm","part":{"id":"prt_1c8fa058a001cRRPA1rL3e1IQP","messageID":"msg_1c8f9d573001ffMPpxzTUYPUsc","sessionID":"ses_e370b5ae4ffe9rbeud9vtYO6Dm","type":"text","text":"<think>No browser available. I'll do a careful static review of the frontend instead, plus a jsdom-based smoke test would require installing jsdom (npm). Let me consider: installing jsdom as a devDependency for a test would pollute the project. I could install it in a temp dir... but /tmp/opencode writes are denied by the permission rule (the earlier writes to /tmp/opencode were denied). Hmm, the rule denied writing to /tmp/opencode/test_api.py. Interesting — the allow pattern for /tmp/opencode is listed but the deny took precedence? The error said the rule prevents this specific tool call. Whatever — I'll avoid /tmp writes.\n\nAlternative: install jsdom in /workspace (npm install --no-save jsdom) temporarily and run a smoke test that renders the App with react-dom/server or jsdom. Since React 19's `react-dom/server` can render to string in Node — I could do a quick SSR smoke test to catch runtime errors in components (though useEffect doesn't run in SSR). That's a decent sanity check.\n\nActually, a simpler and valuable check: render the app with react-dom/server (renderToString) via a small script run with vite's SSR build or tsx? That requires compiling TSX. I could use `npx vite build --ssr` on a small entry file. Hmm, this adds complexity.\n\nAlternative approach: use `npm i --no-save jsdom` + esbuild (already a vite dependency) to transform TSX and run in jsdom. Let me think about value vs. effort. The main risks in the frontend:\n1. Runtime errors from typos/undefined access — mostly caught by TS strict checking (which passed).\n2. Router setup issues — standard.\n3. Tailwind classes not applying — cosmetic.\n\nTS strict passed, so type-level issues are minimal. A jsdom smoke test would catch things like `useAuth` outside provider, etc.\n\nLet me do a lightweight jsdom test: install jsdom with --no-save (won't be in package.json/lock), write a test script that:\n- creates a jsdom window with the built HTML? Actually simpler: use esbuild (bundled with vite) to bundle a test entry that imports the app and renders into jsdom, then assert the DOM contains expected text after data loads (mocking fetch).\n\nThat's a fair amount of work but valuable for verifying the frontend actually works. Let me try a pragmatic version:\n\n1. `npm i --no-save jsdom` in frontend (adds to node_modules only; --no-save prevents package.json/lock changes — need to verify the lock file isn't modified; --no-save does not modify package-lock.json? Actually npm 7+ with --no-save does not write package.json but may still update package-lock.json. I'll back up package-lock.json and restore it after, then verify with git status/diff.)\n\n2. Write /workspace/frontend/smoke_test.mjs that:\n   - sets up jsdom global (window, document, navigator, localStorage)\n   - stubs fetch to return canned API responses\n   - imports the source TSX via a bundling step... needs compilation.\n\nAlternative to bundling: use vite's `build` in SSR/lib mode for a test entry, then run the output in node with jsdom globals. Vite can build multiple entries with lib mode. Let me create a temp entry file `src/__smoke.tsx` that renders the App into document.getElementById('root') and exposes window.__RESULT. Then `npx vite build --ssr src/__smoke.tsx --outDir smoke-out` and run it in node with jsdom.\n\nHmm, but SSR builds don't run effects (no DOM). I need a client-side bundle. I can build with vite in lib mode with formats es and then run in jsdom.\n\nSimpler: use esbuild directly (it's available as a vite dependency in node_modules/esbuild) to bundle the smoke entry to a CJS/ESM file, then run it in Node with jsdom globals set up first.\n\nLet me do that:\n\n```js\n// smoke.mjs\nimport { JSDOM } from 'jsdom';\nconst dom = new JSDOM('<!DOCTYPE html><div id=\"root\"></div>', { url: 'http://localhost/', pretendToBeVisual: true });\nglobal.window = dom.window; global.document = dom.window.document; global.navigator = dom.window.navigator;\nglobal.HTMLElement = dom.window.HTMLElement; ...\n```\n\nReact 19 + jsdom should work.\n\nThen dynamic import of the bundled smoke file which imports React, ReactDOM/client, App, and renders.\n\nfetch: jsdom doesn't provide fetch (Node 18+ has global fetch, but our bundle would use window.fetch?). In the app I call global `fetch` — in the bundle it resolves to globalThis.fetch. I'll stub globalThis.fetch with a mock that serves the API endpoints from a fake dataset.\n\nThen after render, wait a tick, and check document.body.innerHTML contains \"Living room\" etc.\n\nThis is a solid verification. Let me do it, then clean up (remove the smoke files and jsdom from node_modules — node_modules isn't in git anyway; but package-lock.json must stay unchanged).\n\nLet me check git status first to see the repo state.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794373191050,"end":1794373191067}}}
