{"type":"text","timestamp":1794373293903,"sessionID":"ses_e370b5ae4ffe9rbeud9vtYO6Dm","part":{"id":"prt_1c8fb9734001RscrVdYvOUj5FC","messageID":"msg_1c8fb83d4001nq27cp75V5tiMu","sessionID":"ses_e370b5ae4ffe9rbeud9vtYO6Dm","type":"text","text":"<think>All frontend flows verified. Now let me clean up the temporary test files and do the Docker build.\n\nCleanup:\n- /workspace/test_api_tmp.py, /workspace/test_live_tmp.py\n- /workspace/frontend/smoke_test.mjs, smoke_interactions.mjs, smoke_delete.mjs, src/smoke_entry.tsx, smoke-out/\n- /workspace/data-test/\n- /workspace/server.log\n- backend/__pycache__/\n- data/ (created by the local server run) — I'll wipe the DB but keep the empty data dir? The Dockerfile creates `data`. Locally, the .gitignore will exclude data/*.db. Let me delete data/ entirely from the workspace (the app recreates it).\n\nAlso uninstall jsdom? node_modules isn't tracked; but `npm ci` in Docker installs from the lock file, so node_modules content doesn't matter. Still, let me remove jsdom from node_modules to keep things clean... `npm ci` would wipe node_modules anyway. I'll just run `npm ci`? That would take time but ensures the lock file is consistent with package.json. Actually let me verify `npm ci` works with the current lock file — important because the Docker build runs `npm ci`! If the lock file is out of sync with package.json, `npm ci` fails and the Docker build breaks. Let me test `npm ci` locally (it removes node_modules and reinstalls).\n\nLet me first check that the lock file has the new deps (react-router-dom, tailwindcss, typescript, @types/*).\n\nThen run npm ci to validate.\n\nAlso I should double check: does the Docker build's `npm ci` work offline? In the Docker build environment, network access to the npm registry should be available (the sandbox has registry access). The Dockerfile is given, so presumably it works.\n\nLet me now clean up and run npm ci.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794373293876,"end":1794373293899}}}
